<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=portland-cement">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portland cement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ordinary Portland cement, OPC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portland cement is a hydraulic cement made by heating limestone and clay minerals in a kiln to form clinker, then grinding it with a little gypsum; it is the most common cement and the basic ingredient of concrete and mortar. Joseph Aspdin patented it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1824 and named it after Portland stone, quarried on the Isle of Portland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. It grew out of the natural cements made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the middle of the 18th century, John Smeaton’s experiments of 1756 and Louis Vicat’s artificial hydraulic lime. His son William Aspdin improved it in the 1840s at Rotherhithe, and the rotary kiln, patented in 1885, allowed continuous manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portland-cement.jpg" alt="Portland cement" width="330" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KVDP · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portland_Cement_Bags.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1824</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Joseph Aspdin · William Aspdin</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The name Portland cement was recorded in a directory of 1823 with a William Lockwood. Aspdin’s cement has been called a proto-Portland cement: his son William is regarded as the inventor of the modern form, and Isaac Charles Johnson claimed to be its real father.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">Reinforced concrete<span>1853</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=portland-cement">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
